Learn by Examples.Branching for a Feature:
Continuing from [Starting the Project](/git/0.0.0/learn-by-examples/starting-the-project/):
`wordcount` can count words, but not lines or characters. That's a
self-contained feature — exactly what a branch is for.

## 1. Why branch at all

`main` should stay in a state you'd be comfortable shipping at any moment.
A multi-commit feature is, by definition, *not* that — it's incomplete
until the last commit lands. Branching gives the incomplete work a name
of its own, so `main` doesn't have to see it until it's ready.

```
$ git switch -c feature/line-and-char-count
Switched to a new branch 'feature/line-and-char-count'
```

`git switch -c` creates the branch and checks it out in one step. It's
the modern replacement for `git checkout -b`, which still works
identically but also does several other unrelated things — see
[Branching & Merging](/git/0.0.0/everyday-git/branching-&-merging/) for the full picture of
why `switch` and `restore` were split out of `checkout`.

```
$ git branch
* feature/line-and-char-count
  main
```

The `*` marks the branch you're currently on. Nothing about the *files*
changed by branching — `bin/wordcount.dart` looks exactly like it did on
`main`, because the new branch simply points at the same commit
(`4b7e991`) that `main` does. They'll only diverge once you commit.

## 2. Building the feature, one commit at a time

```
$ cat > bin/wordcount.dart << 'EOF'
import 'dart:io';

class Counts {
  Counts(this.words, this.lines, this.chars);
  final int words;
  final int lines;
  final int chars;
}

Counts count(String text) {
  final words = text.trim().isEmpty
      ? 0
      : text.trim().split(RegExp(r'\s+')).length;
  final lines = text.isEmpty ? 0 : text.split('\n').length;
  final chars = text.length;
  return Counts(words, lines, chars);
}

void main(List<String> args) {
  if (args.isEmpty) {
    stderr.writeln('usage: wordcount <file>');
    exit(64);
  }
  final text = File(args.first).readAsStringSync();
  final c = count(text);
  print('${c.lines} ${c.words} ${c.chars} ${args.first}');
}
EOF
```

```
$ git add bin/wordcount.dart
$ git commit -m "Add line and character counting"
[feature/line-and-char-count 1c9d2ee] Add line and character counting
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)
```

While working, check that the output actually looks right before moving
on — this is also a good moment to notice how a diff shows *only* what
you touched:

```
$ echo "hello there
friend" > sample.txt
$ dart run bin/wordcount.dart sample.txt
2 3 18 sample.txt
```

Add a second commit for a `--words-only` flag, keeping the two ideas
separate so either can be reverted independently later if needed:

```
$ cat > bin/wordcount.dart << 'EOF'
import 'dart:io';

class Counts {
  Counts(this.words, this.lines, this.chars);
  final int words;
  final int lines;
  final int chars;
}

Counts count(String text) {
  final words = text.trim().isEmpty
      ? 0
      : text.trim().split(RegExp(r'\s+')).length;
  final lines = text.isEmpty ? 0 : text.split('\n').length;
  final chars = text.length;
  return Counts(words, lines, chars);
}

void main(List<String> args) {
  var wordsOnly = false;
  final rest = <String>[];
  for (final a in args) {
    if (a == '--words-only') {
      wordsOnly = true;
    } else {
      rest.add(a);
    }
  }
  if (rest.isEmpty) {
    stderr.writeln('usage: wordcount [--words-only] <file>');
    exit(64);
  }
  final text = File(rest.first).readAsStringSync();
  final c = count(text);
  if (wordsOnly) {
    print(c.words);
  } else {
    print('${c.lines} ${c.words} ${c.chars} ${rest.first}');
  }
}
EOF
$ git add bin/wordcount.dart
$ git commit -m "Add --words-only flag"
[feature/line-and-char-count 7aa10f4] Add --words-only flag
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
```

## 3. Comparing the branch to main

Before merging anything, look at exactly what the branch would bring in:

```
$ git log --oneline main..feature/line-and-char-count
7aa10f4 Add --words-only flag
1c9d2ee Add line and character counting
```

`main..feature/line-and-char-count` means "commits reachable from the
second ref but not the first" — precisely the two commits this branch
adds. `git diff main...feature/line-and-char-count` (three dots) shows
the same thing as an actual diff, comparing against the point where the
branches split rather than main's current tip.

```
$ git switch main
Switched to branch 'main'
$ cat bin/wordcount.dart | head -5
import 'dart:io';

int countWords(String text) {
  final trimmed = text.trim();
  if (trimmed.isEmpty) return 0;
```

Notice `main`'s copy of the file switched back instantly to the old,
simpler version — switching branches swaps the working directory to
match whatever that branch's last commit recorded. No changes were lost;
they're safely committed on `feature/line-and-char-count` and will
reappear the moment you switch back to it.

Continue to [Merging and a Real Conflict](/git/0.0.0/learn-by-examples/merging-and-a-real-conflict/),
where this feature branch merges into `main` — and a second branch,
started at the same time, doesn't merge so cleanly.

Learn by Examples.Merging and a Real Conflict:
Continuing from [Branching for a Feature](/git/0.0.0/learn-by-examples/branching-for-a-feature/).
The line/char-count feature is done. Merge it in — then watch a second
branch, edited by a teammate at the same time, collide with it.

## 1. A clean merge

```
$ git switch main
Switched to branch 'main'
$ git merge feature/line-and-char-count
Updating 4b7e991..7aa10f4
Fast-forward
 bin/wordcount.dart | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)
```

`Fast-forward` means `main` hadn't moved at all since the branch split
off, so Git didn't need to create a merge commit — it just slid `main`'s
pointer forward to the feature branch's tip. `git log --oneline --graph`
shows a straight line, not a fork:

```
$ git log --oneline --graph
* 7aa10f4 Add --words-only flag
* 1c9d2ee Add line and character counting
* 4b7e991 Implement word counting
* 8f1a2c3 Initial commit: project skeleton
```

The feature branch has done its job and can be deleted — the commits
themselves are safe on `main` now, only the branch *label* goes away:

```
$ git branch -d feature/line-and-char-count
Deleted branch feature/line-and-char-count (was 7aa10f4).
```

## 2. Two branches touching the same lines

Meanwhile, suppose you'd also started a second branch from the same
starting point (`4b7e991`), to add a `--help` flag:

```
$ git switch -c feature/help-flag 4b7e991
Switched to a new branch 'feature/help-flag'
```

```
$ cat > bin/wordcount.dart << 'EOF'
import 'dart:io';

int countWords(String text) {
  final trimmed = text.trim();
  if (trimmed.isEmpty) return 0;
  return trimmed.split(RegExp(r'\s+')).length;
}

void main(List<String> args) {
  if (args.contains('--help')) {
    print('usage: wordcount <file>');
    return;
  }
  if (args.isEmpty) {
    stderr.writeln('usage: wordcount <file>');
    exit(64);
  }
  final text = File(args.first).readAsStringSync();
  print(countWords(text));
}
EOF
$ git add bin/wordcount.dart
$ git commit -m "Add --help flag"
[feature/help-flag e2f5a10] Add --help flag
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)
```

This branch was created from the *old* single-function version of
`main()`, before the line/char-count rewrite landed — it has no idea
that function has since changed shape entirely. Try to merge it into
today's `main`:

```
$ git switch main
Switched to branch 'main'
$ git merge feature/help-flag
Auto-merging bin/wordcount.dart
CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in bin/wordcount.dart
Automatic merge failed; fix conflicts and then commit the result
```

## 3. Reading and resolving the conflict

```
$ git status
On branch main
You have unmerged paths.
  (fix conflicts and run "git commit")
  (use "git merge --abort" to abort the merge)

Unmerged paths:
  (use "git add <file>..." to mark resolution)
        both modified:   bin/wordcount.dart
```

Open the file — Git has inserted conflict markers exactly where the two
histories disagree:

```dart
void main(List<String> args) {
<<<<<<< HEAD
  var wordsOnly = false;
  final rest = <String>[];
  for (final a in args) {
    if (a == '--words-only') {
      wordsOnly = true;
    } else {
      rest.add(a);
    }
  }
  if (rest.isEmpty) {
    stderr.writeln('usage: wordcount [--words-only] <file>');
    exit(64);
  }
=======
  if (args.contains('--help')) {
    print('usage: wordcount <file>');
    return;
  }
  if (args.isEmpty) {
    stderr.writeln('usage: wordcount <file>');
    exit(64);
  }
>>>>>>> feature/help-flag
```

`HEAD` is `main`'s side (the version with line/word/char counting and
`--words-only`); `feature/help-flag` is the incoming side. Both are
right, and both need to survive — this is a case for combining them by
hand, not just picking one side:

```dart
void main(List<String> args) {
  if (args.contains('--help')) {
    print('usage: wordcount [--words-only] <file>');
    return;
  }
  var wordsOnly = false;
  final rest = <String>[];
  for (final a in args) {
    if (a == '--words-only') {
      wordsOnly = true;
    } else {
      rest.add(a);
    }
  }
  if (rest.isEmpty) {
    stderr.writeln('usage: wordcount [--words-only] <file>');
    exit(64);
  }
```

Remove every `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, and `>>>>>>>` marker — Git will not do
this for you, and a stray marker left behind is a common source of "why
won't this compile" moments right after a merge. Then verify it actually
runs before trusting it:

```
$ dart run bin/wordcount.dart --help
usage: wordcount [--words-only] <file>
```

Stage the resolved file and finish the merge:

```
$ git add bin/wordcount.dart
$ git commit
[main 9c3b7a2] Merge branch 'feature/help-flag'
```

Running `git commit` with no `-m` here opens your editor with a
pre-filled merge message (see [Configuration](/git/0.0.0/reference/configuration/) for
setting which editor opens) — normally fine to accept as-is, though you
can add a note about how the conflict was resolved if it wasn't obvious.

```
$ git log --oneline --graph
*   9c3b7a2 Merge branch 'feature/help-flag'
|\
| * e2f5a10 Add --help flag
* | 7aa10f4 Add --words-only flag
* | 1c9d2ee Add line and character counting
|/
* 4b7e991 Implement word counting
* 8f1a2c3 Initial commit: project skeleton
```

Unlike the first merge, this one really did need a merge commit
(`9c3b7a2`) — it has two parents, one from each branch, which is exactly
why the graph shows a diamond instead of a straight line. See
[Branching & Merging](/git/0.0.0/everyday-git/branching-&-merging/) for when a
fast-forward is and isn't possible, and `git merge --abort` as an escape
hatch if a conflict resolution ever goes sideways mid-merge.

```
$ git branch -d feature/help-flag
Deleted branch feature/help-flag (was e2f5a10).
```

Continue to [Fixing Mistakes](/git/0.0.0/learn-by-examples/fixing-mistakes/),
where a bad commit, a bad `git add`, and a bad merge all get undone —
each with the tool actually suited to it.

Learn by Examples.Stashing to Switch Context:
Continuing from [Fixing Mistakes](/git/0.0.0/learn-by-examples/fixing-mistakes/).
Midway through a new feature, an unrelated bug report comes in that needs
a fix *right now*, on `main`, before the feature is anywhere near ready
to commit.

## 1. Caught mid-change

```
$ git switch -c feature/stdin-support
Switched to a new branch 'feature/stdin-support'
```

Partway through wiring up stdin support:

```
$ cat > bin/wordcount.dart << 'EOF'
import 'dart:io';

class Counts {
  Counts(this.words, this.lines, this.chars);
  final int words;
  final int lines;
  final int chars;
}

Counts count(String text) {
  final words = text.trim().isEmpty
      ? 0
      : text.trim().split(RegExp(r'\s+')).length;
  final lines = text.isEmpty ? 0 : text.split('\n').length;
  final chars = text.length;
  return Counts(words, lines, chars);
}

void main(List<String> args) {
  // TODO: still needs to read from stdin when no file is given
  if (args.contains('--help')) {
    print('usage: wordcount [--words-only] <file>');
    return;
  }
EOF
```

That's an incomplete, non-compiling file — exactly the kind of thing you
don't want to commit, but also don't want to lose:

```
$ git status
On branch feature/stdin-support
Changes not staged for commit:
  (use "git add <file>..." to update what will be committed)
        modified:   bin/wordcount.dart
```

## 2. Stashing it away

```
$ git stash push -m "WIP: stdin support, incomplete"
Saved working directory and index state On feature/stdin-support: \
  a1b2c3d WIP: stdin support, incomplete
```

```
$ git status
On branch feature/stdin-support
nothing to commit, working tree clean
```

The working directory is clean again — as if the change never
happened — but it hasn't been lost, only set aside:

```
$ git stash list
stash@{0}: On feature/stdin-support: WIP: stdin support, incomplete
```

## 3. Fixing the bug on main

```
$ git switch main
Switched to branch 'main'
```

Notice `git switch` refused nothing here — because the stash already
tucked the messy change away, `main`'s checkout is unaffected by it.

```
$ git switch -c hotfix/empty-file-crash
Switched to a new branch 'hotfix/empty-file-crash'
```

```
$ touch empty.txt
$ dart run bin/wordcount.dart empty.txt
0 0 0 empty.txt
```

That one's actually fine. But a genuinely missing file crashes ugly:

```
$ dart run bin/wordcount.dart nope.txt
Unhandled exception:
PathNotFoundException: Cannot open file, path = 'nope.txt' ...
```

Fix it properly:

```
$ cat bin/wordcount.dart
```

```dart
import 'dart:io';

class Counts {
  Counts(this.words, this.lines, this.chars);
  final int words;
  final int lines;
  final int chars;
}

Counts count(String text) {
  final words = text.trim().isEmpty
      ? 0
      : text.trim().split(RegExp(r'\s+')).length;
  final lines = text.isEmpty ? 0 : text.split('\n').length;
  final chars = text.length;
  return Counts(words, lines, chars);
}

void main(List<String> args) {
  if (args.contains('--help')) {
    print('usage: wordcount [--words-only] <file>');
    return;
  }
  var wordsOnly = false;
  final rest = <String>[];
  for (final a in args) {
    if (a == '--words-only') {
      wordsOnly = true;
    } else {
      rest.add(a);
    }
  }
  if (rest.isEmpty) {
    stderr.writeln('usage: wordcount [--words-only] <file>');
    exit(64);
  }
  final path = rest.first;
  if (!File(path).existsSync()) {
    stderr.writeln('wordcount: no such file: $path');
    exit(66);
  }
  final text = File(path).readAsStringSync();
  final c = count(text);
  if (wordsOnly) {
    print(c.words);
  } else {
    print('${c.lines} ${c.words} ${c.chars} $path');
  }
}
```

```
$ git add bin/wordcount.dart
$ git commit -m "Exit cleanly when input file is missing"
[hotfix/empty-file-crash b3c4d5e] Exit cleanly when input file is missing
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)
```

```
$ git switch main
$ git merge hotfix/empty-file-crash
Updating 5e4d3c2..b3c4d5e
Fast-forward
 bin/wordcount.dart | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)
$ git branch -d hotfix/empty-file-crash
Deleted branch hotfix/empty-file-crash (was b3c4d5e).
```

## 4. Resuming the stashed work

```
$ git switch feature/stdin-support
Switched to branch 'feature/stdin-support'
```

This branch was created *before* the hotfix existed, so it's now missing
that fix. Bring `main` up to date on this branch before continuing — see
[Rebasing & Cherry-Picking](/git/0.0.0/advanced/rebasing-&-cherry-picking/) for `rebase`
as an alternative to the merge shown here:

```
$ git merge main
Updating a1b2c3d..b3c4d5e
Fast-forward
 bin/wordcount.dart | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)
```

Now restore the stashed work on top of it:

```
$ git stash pop
On branch feature/stdin-support
Changes not staged for commit:
  (use "git add <file>..." to update what will be committed)
        modified:   bin/wordcount.dart

Dropped refs/stash@{0}
```

```
$ git stash list
```

(empty — `pop` applied the stash and removed it from the list in one
step; `git stash apply` would have applied it but kept it listed, useful
if you might want the same stash again on a different branch.)

Finish the feature:

```
$ cat >> bin/wordcount.dart << 'EOF'
// stdin support continues here...
EOF
$ git add bin/wordcount.dart
$ git commit -m "Read from stdin when no file argument is given"
[feature/stdin-support c4d5e6f] Read from stdin when no file argument is given
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
```

## 5. When a stash pop conflicts

If the hotfix and the stashed change had touched the same lines, `git
stash pop` would have produced conflict markers exactly like a merge
does — resolve them the same way as on
[Merging and a Real Conflict](/git/0.0.0/learn-by-examples/merging-and-a-real-conflict/),
then `git add` the resolved files. In that case the stash is deliberately
**not** dropped automatically (only a clean pop drops it), so if the
resolution goes wrong you can `git checkout -- .` to bail out and the
stash is still sitting safely in the list to try again.

